=== PART 3. OPTION 2 -- MARGINALISE THE SIGMA UNCERTAINTY (Student-t) ===
measured sd(log sigma) = 0.614  ->  nu = 2.12, scale s = 9.87 permil
nu that small is very heavy-tailed -- near-Cauchy. That is not a modelling
flourish, it is what a 17x bracket on sigma actually implies.

deviation being priced                 Gaussian    Student-t
kz runaway, 62.6 permil                   20.08         4.67
10 permil                                  0.51         0.62
20 permil                                  2.05         1.68
30 permil                                  4.62         2.62
50 permil                                 12.83         4.02

"HOLDS kz" threshold (the build script's z > 3) = 4.50 log-units.
  Gaussian at s = 9.87:  20.08  -> HOLDS
  Student-t nu = 2.12:   4.67  -> HOLDS

READ THAT PAIR CAREFULLY -- I EXPECTED OPTION 2 TO FAIL HERE AND IT DOES NOT.
The t still HOLDS, at 4.67 against a threshold of 4.50. What it does is
cut the runaway penalty 4.3x (20.1 -> 4.67) and leave a 4 % margin where
there was a 4x one. So the objection to option 2 is NOT that it breaks the
term's founding purpose -- it is that it removes all the margin on it, and
the margin is what would let the term REPLACE the kz bracket rather than sit
behind it. Under option 2 the bracket stays load-bearing indefinitely.

CAVEAT ON THE COMPARISON: the z > 3 criterion was written for a Gaussian, so
scoring a t against a Gaussian-derived threshold is like-for-like only in the
operational sense that matters here -- how many log-units the objective pays
for the runaway. It is not a tail probability.


